package pages

templ SyntaxMatchPage() {
	<h1>Match</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>In addition to <code>if</code> control flow, ghoti has match expressions</li>
		<li>Match expressions can easily switch over enum values, but are also helpful when working with unions</li>
		<li>
			When matching a union, you must use a capture group with each match arm
			<ul>
				<li>This will capture the active field value of the union</li>
				<li>This can also be discarded using an underscore <code>_</code></li>
				<li>There is no concept of a modifier with this capture, with access modifiers being dependent on the match object/the union's field types</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			Match expressions must always be exhaustive
			<ul>
				<li>
					To catch all arms that were not explicitly listed, you can use a catch-all branch where the LHS of the branch is the underscore <code>_</code> token
					<ul>
						<li>This branch may capture the scrutinee (<code>_ =&gt; |v| v * 2</code>); on a tagged union the capture is the whole union, not a payload</li>
						<li>This branch may come anywhere in the match arm list</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
				<li>Similar to <code>for</code> and <code>while</code> non-break, the catch-all clause is a restricted statement, meaning it can only be a:</li>
				<li>Jump statement (return, break, continue)</li>
				<li>Expression statement</li>
				<li>Block statement</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Arms of match expressions must be comma separated and there may be a trailing comma</li>
		<li>Loops are expressions and must be terminated by a semicolon</li>
		<li>
			An arm may list several comma-separated patterns; the arm is taken when the scrutinee matches any of them
			<ul>
				<li>A single capture is shared by every listed pattern</li>
				<li>For a union or enum, every listed variant must carry the same payload type to bind a capture</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			A range may be used as a pattern: <code>lo..hi</code> (exclusive) or <code>lo..=hi</code> (inclusive)
			<ul>
				<li>Endpoints may be any expression, including runtime values (<code>a..c</code>, <code>5..=arr.len</code>)</li>
				<li>Compile-time ranges count toward exhaustiveness, so <code>0..=127, 128..=255</code> covers a <code>u8</code>. A range with a runtime endpoint covers nothing</li>
				<li>A range may leave off an end to reach the limit of the type: <code>1..</code>, <code>..0</code></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>{ "match comptime (x) { ... }" }</code> selects its arm at compile time, exactly like <code>{ "if comptime" }</code>
			<ul>
				<li>The scrutinee must be compile-time known</li>
				<li>Only the selected arm is type-checked; the others may reference undeclared names</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Nested patterns</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			A pattern can reach through a value and bind the parts it finds
			<ul>
				<li>A union variant takes one pattern for its payload: <code>{ ".ok(.some(|raw|))" }</code>. A bare <code>.ok</code> means <code>.ok(_)</code></li>
				<li>A struct pattern names fields and ignores the rest: <code>{ ".{ .x = 0, .y = |y| }" }</code></li>
				<li>A fixed array lists every element: <code>{ ".{ |first|, _, |last| }" }</code></li>
				<li>A slice matches its <code>.len</code> and <code>.ptr</code>, or a string literal</li>
				<li>Any of these can name their type: <code>{ "Shape.circle(|r|)" }</code>, <code>{ "Point{ .x = |x| }" }</code></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li><code>|name|</code> binds what's there, in any capture form (<code>|&amp;mut v|</code>), and <code>_</code> ignores it</li>
		<li><code>{ "pattern |name|" }</code> tests a value and binds the whole of it. On a variant with a payload, write <code>{ ".ok(_) |whole|" }</code>, since <code>{ ".ok(|v|)" }</code> binds the payload</li>
		<li>Patterns look through a <code>&amp;T</code>, but never a <code>^T</code>: a pointer can only be captured or compared with a constant</li>
		<li>Values nested inside a pattern must be known at compile time. Only a top-level pattern may be a runtime expression</li>
		<li>Every alternative of an arm must bind the same names with the same types</li>
		<li>The first arm that matches wins, with <code>_</code> always tried last. An arm an earlier one fully covers is an error</li>
		<li>
			A guard adds a condition: <code>{ ".{ .x = |x| } if x > 0 => x" }</code>
			<ul>
				<li>When it's false, matching carries on with the arms after it</li>
				<li>A guarded arm never counts toward exhaustiveness</li>
				<li>A guard can read through <code>|&amp;mut v|</code> but can't write through it</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre><code class="language-ghoti">{ `match (r) {
    .ok(.some(|raw|)) => use(raw),
    .ok(.none), .err => fallback(),
}

match (p) {
    .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 } => origin(),
    .{ .x = |x|, .y = |y| } if x == y => diagonal(x),
    .{ .x = |x|, .y = |y| } => general(x, y),
}

match (s) {
    "main" => entry(),
    .{ .len = 1.. } |name| => other(name),
    _ => empty(),
}` }</code></pre>
	<pre><code class="language-ghoti">{ "match (a) { 2 => { c; }, _ => d }; // Standard match on a non-union value\nmatch (a) { .b => |b| { c; } }; // Match on a union value that has a field `b`\nmatch (n) { 1, 2, 3 => |v| v * 2, 4..8 => 0, _ => -1 }; // Multi-value and range arms\nmatch comptime (@TypeOf(x)) { i32 => small(), _ => big() }; // Compile-time arm selection" }</code></pre>
}
